A lazy object is created by calling the LazySingleton<T> class's Value property. Laziness
guarantees that an object is not created until the Value property is called. Once created, the
singleton implementation ensures that the same object is returned on subsequent calls. A
null check on _value avoids creating a lock on subsequent calls, thereby saving some
memory I/O operations and improving performance.
